#use(std/code)

#section{Indented blocks}::

A call alone on its line takes the following indented block as its body.
Indentation is #strong{tabs only}, exactly one tab per nesting level.

#code(lang: "plain")
	before
	#emph
		first level
		#strong
			second level
		first again
	after

Live:

before
#emph
	first level
	#strong
		second level
	first again
after

Blank lines never close a block — only a non-blank line with fewer tabs does.
Inside a markup block body a blank line is a paragraph break:

#code(lang: "plain")
	#blockquote
		first para

		second para
	tail

Live:

#blockquote
	first para

	second para
tail

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: A line starting with spaces at a structure position is a reader error; so is jumping more than one level deeper at once.
	#item: The block form requires a #emph{bare} call — #code{#name} or #code{#name(args)} only. A call with an inline #code|{{body}}| or #code{: text} followed by a deeper line is an error, not a guessed block.
	#item: Consequence: #code|{#section{Title}}| + an indented block is #emph{not a form} — the title brace disqualifies the block body. Use #code|{#section{Title}::}| instead.
	#item: Raw block bodies (e.g. #code{#code}) strip one leading tab per level and keep everything after verbatim, including leading spaces — see the code page.
